<<~ ahu #/the-attractor>>

!! The attractor ~ STABLE FOUNDING

''STABLE FOUNDING'' names the state the house grows toward (operator-ruled). Once a Lararium stands, its
base state reads STABLE: a PRIVATE personal Nexus for the operator's own devices, and a FOUNDED PERSONA
ZERO. Everything else flows downstream of it — contracting into other Nexuses, inviting the operator's own
PersonaGroup devices, and hosting guests who walk.

This carrier holds the road there: the rulings that shape it (#/rulings), the design decisions the hosted
walk settled and their reasons (#/decisions), the re-found's slices in dependency order (#/the-road), the
fresh start (#/fresh-start), and the questions the operator still owns (#/open). The live wounds and the
vitals read in `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/er-handoff`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/rulings>>

!! Rulings ~ the operator's, as approved

!!! The Stable state and its consequences
* ''Every Nexus has a charter.'' The household stands as its own kahu cabal: a CABAL-OF-ONE reads pono, because humans act as pluralities anyway. One "I" may hold several PersonaGroups, independent or aware of each other. A Nexus grows by adding seats, never by changing kind.
* ''Guests walk the private Nexus.'' Planes stay persona-scoped, so a guest sees its own documents plus the public and infrastructure shelf.
* ''Add-a-face (A).'' The daemon writes the public half; the terminal seals the recovery share; seating stays a separate act.
* ''Every leaf starts at the floor with its own root.'' A human may later JOIN a leaf to a PersonaGroup so its devices sync as one fleet; joining never takes the device's own root away.
* ''Founding persona zero belongs to standing'', never to a later verb. A capable vessel founds its private Nexus, so a "vessel with no charter" reads Mu.
* ''Stable folds into the re-found'' as its core.
* ''Forks report per board'': every presenter names every fork on the board it reads (the KERI watcher shape).

!!! Custody ~ the Vessel KEK
* One Vessel KEK (VK), minted at the Stable founding, lives only inside LUKS-style keyslots, each a Clevis `sss` tree. Pin kinds: passphrase · paper (SLIP-39) · ember · tpm (one share only).
* ''EMBER'' names the Tang-shaped, per-relation device pin; it holds no share of VK. Only the blinded points cross on unlock.
* ''A backup route stands at every tier.'' Every keyslot tree, household or civic, keeps a human route (passphrase or paper); bind refuses a tree without one. Embers never stand as the only way in.
* ''The floor key keeps the vessel's public name''; the sealed custody root derives the veil and the sovereign caps. The veil change stays destroy-class.
* ''Unbind offers a VK rotation loudly'', every time, never folded in silently.
* ''Locality law'': an unattended ember answers on the local link only; a relay pin wants an attending hand. The vessel rests at the floor and raises when t embers answer; the raise lapses by non-renewal.
* ''HOT ⊥ COLD'': VK and keyhive state stay hot; the root, the recovery shares and the next-root seed unwrap per act.
* Measuring hardware x-only ECDH (phone enclaves) before trusting an enclave-held ember: approved, deferred.

!!! The hosted walk (F1)
* ''(c′) adopted.'' The HOST's gate reads and burns invites. The gate sorts every socket into same-operator · contracted · walker · stranger; under PRIVATE a stranger meets silence before any session frame. The guest carries its hosting grant; the hearth stores nothing about it. Invites mint by blind POPRF evaluation, so who-invited-whom stays non-computable. Revocation runs by kapae of the guest's leaf, by non-renewal, or by an epoch roll. Sybils meet tolerance, never detection.
* ''The inviter's face never signs an invite'': that signature would link the inviter at redemption. The hearth's blind evaluation carries the proof instead.
* ''V — vesting by class.'' A guest the HOST invites holds floor 1 at once; a guest a WALKER invites vests at the host's next roll. The roll reads as the household's consent to grow, and purpose labels separate the two classes without naming the minter.
* ''W — the hearth carries a walker's own documents'' as sealed ciphertext (carry ⊥ read), under a quota per guest. The PersonaGroup holds the durable copy; hearth carriage stays a convenience, never a backup.
* ''W reclaim.'' Quota pressure alone triggers it; lapse reads as a RATIO against the guest's own rhythm between contacts, so the host's roll cadence cancels; the most-lapsed-for-itself goes first; notice on the guest's next contact comes before any discard. The ruling sets aside counting the host's rolls: it measures the host's diligence, and diligence confers no title.
* ''N — a node may walk.'' The sorter sorts by proven relation, never by device type.

!!! The cleanup of the hosted walk (B1)
* ''The worn face hosts and walks.'' No roster order picks anything; a vessel wearing no face hosts and walks nowhere. Before its first hosting act in a Nexus, a roll refuses when the hosting doc holds an act this face signed that no local state holds.
* ''A herm answers the knock.'' A charterless herm answers any proven peer that knocked (`ownPlacePosture`): a waystone by class, never a broken lararium. A lararium reads its charter, and an absent or torn charter reads PRIVATE.
* ''A boot follows `LAR_ROOT`.'' A vessel isolated under `LAR_ROOT` projects into that root, never into the tracked tree: the bags root resolves `LAR_BAGS` → the config's bags resource → `<LAR_ROOT>/bags` → the repo, and the disk projection's mirror roots resolve the named `rootDir` → `LAR_ROOT` → the repo. The isolation instruction names `LAR_ROOT`: the mirror roots never read `LAR_BAGS`, so `LAR_BAGS` alone keeps no in-tree boot out of the tracked tree.
* ''Compartments outrank admission control.'' A walker reads its own hearth's hosting doc, never the carriage board: the carrier set stays a contract ledger read by those who hold contracts.

!!! The fresh start
* ''No pave or wipe command'' — such a command carries too much risk. See #/fresh-start.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/decisions>>

!! Decisions ~ what the hosted walk settled, and why

* ''One sorter, inside the gate, before the verdict.'' Every gate instance (`/ws`, `/oracle`, the membership relay) sorts before `lar:auth-ok`, so posture reads once, at admission, and never again at share time. Two readers of one posture drift.
* ''The knock.'' A gate answers an upgrade only on a path derived from its own key (`gate-knock`), so a dialer holding no pin draws no HTTP 101 — the WireGuard mac1 shape. The path still rides the upgrade in the clear on plain `ws`; full client-proves-first waits for Noise IK (#/the-road, R-WIRE).
* ''Silence, one deadline.'' No denial frame and no close codes: one drop deadline drawn at accept, so verify time leaks nothing and a PRIVATE stranger looks exactly like a failed proof.
* ''The multi-Nexus fold.'' A gate answers strangers if ANY Nexus it stands in reads OPEN. One gate key serves every Nexus a vessel carries, so this rule reduces exactly to per-Nexus posture once each Nexus carries its own wire key — not an interim.
* ''The epoch names the CID of a public hosting act.'' A hearth-picked random id per walker would tag each walker; one signed act on one replicated doc makes a forked epoch surface as a fork. In a one-hearth Nexus the hearth relays its own doc, so a fork there stays undetectable (the CABAL-OF-ONE bound, named in canon).
* ''The grant rides a keyed tag, never a signature.'' Only the hearth verifies it, so a seized phone proves nothing to anyone else (the Lox and Signal Private Group System line).
* ''POPRF over ristretto255 from the pinned `@noble/curves`'': no new dependency. Labelled honestly as Privacy-Pass-shaped, not RFC 9578 wire-conformant.
* ''The wallet mints in full, in one blind batch''; minting on demand would link by timing. One burn marker per lineage per epoch keeps the batch digest, so a retry burns nothing new.
* ''The hearth derives the claim, never stores it, and binds it to the proving leaf.'' The spent-set keeps `n → H(claim, leaf)`: a retry under the same leaf earns the identical grant; a captured claim replayed under another leaf meets silence and destroys nothing; a seizer cannot compute any lineage.
* ''Carriage keys on the guest's PROVEN leaf.'' A hearth-issued stub would read as a bearer capability to another guest's record and as a hearth-minted cross-lineage linker. The leaf already proves on every socket, so keying on it adds no linkage the hearth lacks; a fresh leaf opens a fresh carriage.
* ''A per-hearth hosting doc'' (`hostingDocUrl(N, gate key)`): the smallest board a walker needs — its own hearth's act chain, the same doc for every walker of that hearth, replicated by the Nexus's carriers for the cross-check.
* ''One store door.'' A command that touches the store runs inside the standing vessel over the owner-only socket, else owns the store itself for that moment. A second Repo beside a live holder writes bytes the replica never sees.
* ''No hearth-local deny set.'' Kapae and the roll suffice; a deny set would make a seizable list.
* ''No node carriage.'' A node serves as its own durable store and no story walks its carriage; ruling N holds as a sorter property and W as the hearth's service. Node carry would open a new user story, never owed by N or W.
* ''Hosting stays on the worn persona leaf.'' ROOT-ON-FOUNDER keeps a persona root on one vessel, so one face cannot host from two stores unseen; the first-roll rail surfaces the remaining case.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-road>>

!! The road ~ the re-found, sliced by true dependency

''The organizing law'': identity bytes (the Nexus AID, the device edge, the dyad scope, the seats, the
sentinel) change ONCE, inside one train. ''The owner law'': each slice lands its logic in modules, and one
named owner per step commits the one compose hunk in `open-node-vessel.ts` and `open-browser-vessel.ts`;
every other lane stops at a module boundary. Each lane's first commit carries its canon.

The train: ''R-STORE ‖ R-CUSTODY → R-CHARTER (one AID train) → R-WIRE → R-ENGINE · R-EMBER · R-KAHU'',
with the lanes beside it. The R-ENGINE fold builds in parallel from the start and may land early.

!!! R-STORE ~ one store door for every writer (parallel from the start; no identity bytes)
* Route the remaining direct-store writers through the one store door: `nexus-contract`, `nexus publish`, `edge-kapae`, `nexus-kapae`, `cabal-vouch`, `device-admit`, `persona new N`. Readers route too, so none reads disk the holder has not flushed.
* Delete `nexus refresh`'s throwaway-Repo board-merge bridge — a second door for the live-holder gap once every write routes.
* Land `tools/direct-repo-witness.sh` with the last conversion, so it never lands red: no `new Repo(` in `lararium-node/src/commands/*` beyond the door's own direct branch, with a planted known positive as its control.

!!! R-CUSTODY ~ the Vessel KEK (parallel with R-STORE; no identity bytes)
* VK, keyslots and the four verbs `vault unlock · bind · unbind · status` over the socket; `vault seal|rotate|export|repair` and `LARES_ARCHIVE_PASSPHRASE` retire.
* ''One carrier table'' names every secret at rest — the vessel seed, the persona roots, the recovery shares, the next-root seed (`.vessel-next*.json`, cold), the keyring, the hosting state, the carry scalars, the walk wallet — and the census DERIVES from it, never beside it.
* ''One sealed writer'': sidecar → re-open → compare → rename, never sealing in place; a witness refuses any 0600 file or seal outside the table. The cleartext carrier leaves only after a SEPARATE process opened the sealed one.
* ''The old-shape refusal'' lands first, red-first: the new build refuses a cleartext root with no slot tree, before any write.
* The vessel KEL re-incepts with no wall clock and no version field. Fresh foundings seal from birth; nothing wraps cleartext in place.
* The enumerable carriage rows and the leaf-testability bound close at rest here.

!!! R-CHARTER ~ the one AID train (after R-STORE and R-CUSTODY)
* ''The Stable founding act'': one interactive act under one passphrase (the VK's first slot) yields the place, persona zero, a PRIVATE charter as a cabal-of-one (one seat, t=1, per-seat next digests) and the keyring keyed by `epochCid`, minted at the seat and never at boot.
* ''Charter shape'': per-seat pre-rotation (`nextKeyDigests`, `nextThreshold`) replacing the single next-key commit; the cadence inception cited once by the founding act; the seal-roll successor named (#/open); `epoch0-<sha>` names become `nexus-<sha>`; `genesisSealEpochCid` deleted.
* ''Re-scoped keys'': the chairs re-key to per-Nexus leaves (a root-signing seat breaches Q1-strict); the device edge and the dyad scope commit to the Nexus AID; the sentinel (the MeshCabal keyhive identity) re-keys.
* ''Boards resolve per relation''; the keyring takes deliveries additively and never overwrites the private Nexus's secret; one fleet-join door replaces `device-admit` and `persona admit`; add-a-face (A); the bare primary-path seal import goes; the mirror guard keys on posture.
* The staging harness founds through the one act inside the train, because the e2e set reads red from the first charter-byte change until the founding lands.
* ''Expiry rides the epoch, never the wall clock'' (the F1 pattern: a grant lapses by non-renewal, by an epoch roll, or by its burn marker). Three sites gate on `Date.now()`, and each folds here, where the charter's epoch names the frontier a grant lapses against:
** `persona-admit.ts:120/198/273`: the offer signs a wall-clock `expiry` into the `GrantTranscript`, and both readers gate it on the local clock.
** `cabal-vouch.ts:74/95`, with `cabal-invite.ts:99` and `vouch-board.ts:94`: the vouch signs a 30-day `expiresAt` into the invite bytes and gates the mint on it, so one vouch minted at two instants signs two different byte strings. The invite already carries `boundEpoch`, the frontier it can lapse against.
** `keyhive-identity-slot.ts:125/156`: the unsigned token's `expiresAtMs` gates `verifyDelegation` on the reader's clock. Measured: only tests construct `KeyhiveIdentitySlot`, and no live path calls `delegateCapability` or `verifyDelegation`, so this clock guards nothing live; its expiry gets decided where a live path first wires the slot.

!!! R-WIRE ~ the per-Nexus wire key (after R-CHARTER, so every pin changes once)
* A per-Nexus wire key derived from the sealed custody root and the Nexus AID; one gate per (Nexus, route), so knock, pin, verdict and posture read per Nexus exactly.
* ''Noise IK, proof-first'': the gate emits nothing before a valid first message; the knock becomes the IK pre-message.
* The floor announcement signs under the wire key, folded in here, so gate and announce never sign under two keys.
* One client dial per (hearth, Nexus), so a vessel walks at more than one hearth; until then a second walk hearth refuses aloud.
* The vessel key stops linking a walker's leaves across Nexuses on the wire (`two-maps` clause 5's residual), and the oracle pointer's vessel-key correlation goes with it (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/pronaos#/the-rung-ladder`).
* ''Per-dial transport keys on the sibling channel'': a leaf proves to a herm under a key fresh per dial, and the leaf-peer transcript binds that key in place of the device key the relay stamps, so a herm colluding with a revoked leaf reads no sibling's device key off its own gate (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/identity-slot-policy#/the-persona-group-secret`).
* ''Owed (a), the dialer's name rides the clear'': the dialer's ContactCard crosses in cleartext in `lar:auth` over plain `ws://` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/auth-wire.ts:181-183`) — a live THR_2 leak that Noise IK's encrypted first message cures, the dialer's static riding inside message one instead.
* ''Owed (b), the knock tests offline'': the knock path computes an HMAC of the public gate key (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/gate-knock.ts:36-44`); an observer holding candidate keys tests a sniffed knock offline, so not echoing the key protects only against an observer with no candidates.

!!! R-ENGINE ~ the engine fold, then its offers
* ''Fold'' (parallel from the start): TW5 core and the memetic-wikitext bootstrap fold into `engineCid`; the vocabulary leaves the engine as content; the coal-only seed carries no `schemaVersion`; the island `schema_version` drops its counter.
* ''Offers'' (after R-CHARTER): the engine-offer lineage signs under the cadence threshold, adopted within a k-hop window along `prev`.
* ''Landed early'': the TW5 engine moved to `develop` at `f04b82899` (upstream master plus donation-bound fixes); genesis caught up at `099f2bd42`; `actorSeed` folds only inputs every Lararium shares and boots alike.
* ''Streams ruling owed'': streams leaves genesis in the slice that adopts it as a crossroads Offering; plugins load by recipe listing. Only the Lararium boot set rides genesis.

!!! R-EMBER ~ the ember pin (after R-CHARTER's two-mount join)
* A household's first ember stands on its second device, which needs the two-mount join; the slot tree ships whole in R-CUSTODY and this slice adds a pin kind.

!!! R-KAHU ~ the kahu cabal across vessels
* S1, a quorum whose seats live on different vessels, and L1, the realm root map from the co-signed `keptBy`. Needs only the seat shape.

Beside the train, landed and off this road: leaf↔leaf sync through a herm, session-bound (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/identity-slot-policy#/the-leaf-taxonomy`); the held-out dispatch, driven from a host-built dist with `vessel found` on the runner (`.github/workflows/held-out.yml`).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/fresh-start>>

!! The fresh start ~ by hand, reversible, never a command

NO pave or wipe command stands (operator-ruled): such a command carries too much risk. In early alpha a fresh
START still reads pono when it runs BY HAND: MOVE the old homes aside — rename them, never delete them, so
the move stays reversible — then found fresh on the re-found build. Valuable data lives in git, in the
private `claude-backup` repository, or on `D:`; a vessel home holds nothing that only it holds.

The homes to move: the ones the OLD build's resolvers name — the `~/.lares` home, the XDG data homes for
`lares` and `lararium`, and the state home — asked of the old build at the moment of moving, never
enumerated by hand. The new build's old-shape refusal (#/the-road, R-CUSTODY) keeps an accidental boot
off any home left unmoved. Other Nexuses rejoin through presented admits; every old admit names an
old AID and reads dead by construction.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/open>>

!! Open ~ what the operator still owns

# ''The seal-roll successor's name.'' Mint it inside the standing `seal` family (`seal-seat-roll`), or choose a family word first (`charter-roll`). The name changes with the bytes either way.
# ''A root-holding browser leaf's backup slot.'' A WebCrypto non-extractable slot allows no paper backup in-browser; does "a backup at every tier" require such a leaf to hold a paper or passphrase slot too? Lean: yes for a leaf holding a persona root; a floor-only leaf holds no tree.
# ''The seat's cold next key at founding.'' Arm it at the Stable founding (one sheet, two labelled sections — the VK backup and the seat's next key — each read back), or leave the seat to commit later? Lean: arm it; an unarmed charter never rotates.
# ''A sealed receipt manifest for carriage.'' It would let a root restored from paper re-open its carriage after device loss — and would turn carriage toward the backup W rules it never becomes. The lenses split; re-opening W needs the operator's turned key.
# ''Carriage across leaves.'' A guest-held carry-claim proven at the carry door would let carriage follow a guest across leaves — a second relation and a second domain. Lean: not built.

<<~/ahu>>

